**Scoped, not started — the shape is clear and the session that starts it should not be the one that ends late.**

Read `estiva-id/src/domain/policy.ts` with intent. `evaluateSignPolicy` already has exactly the pattern this ticket asks for, one kind over:

```ts
if (event.kind === KIND_HTTP_AUTH) {
  const url = tagValue(event, 'u')
  if (!url) return refuse('nip98_missing_url', …)
  const allowedByDeployment = deploymentNip98Prefixes.some((p) => url.startsWith(p))
  const allowedByApp = app.nip98UrlPrefixes.some((p) => url.startsWith(p))
  if (!allowedByDeployment || !allowedByApp) return refuse('nip98_url_not_allowed', …)
}
```

Two bounds, intersected: the deployment's outer list and the app's own. An app can narrow itself and never widen past the deployment. That is the shape to copy for `22242`, reading the `relay` tag instead of `u`.

## What it actually needs

1. **`22242` in the `peek` entry** of `src/db/seed.ts` — one line, and **it must not land without item 2**. Granted unqualified, Peek can mint relay-auth for any relay at all.
2. **A `relay`-tag branch in `evaluateSignPolicy`**, mirroring the NIP-98 one.
3. **Somewhere for the allowed relays to come from.** This is the open question and the reason it is not a copy-paste: `nip98UrlPrefixes` is a per-app column (`app_credentials.nip98_url_prefixes`) plus a deployment list. There is no equivalent column for relays, and no `RELAY_URL` in Estiva ID's config at all — I looked. So the decision is whether to reuse `nip98_url_prefixes` (they would in practice hold the same origin), add a column, or derive the bound from the deployment's known relay. **Reusing the NIP-98 column is tempting and probably wrong** — the two are different capabilities and collapsing them means widening one silently widens the other.
4. Tests alongside `seed.test.ts`, and delete the assertion there that currently pins `22242` as *absent* — it exists to make the pairing deliberate and should go, not flip.

## Why it is worth the care

The failure mode this prevents is not loud. `policy.ts` refuses at `/sign`, so a missing scope does not break anything — it silently permits. Nobody would notice until an app authenticated somewhere it should not have.

Note this ticket is also the last thing between Gate 1 and closing, alongside CRO-1'"'"'s `/nip44` endpoints.

**First half of the done-when is met and verified on production. Second half I cannot test.**

Granted in estiva-id#54, merged, and re-seeded by hand on the box just now. The row, read rather than the `seeded 2 app credential(s)` line:

    estiva-peek  {9,7,5,9007,27235,1851,9101,9002,9008,1111,30078,41010,41011,41012}
    estiva-ship  {30850,30851,1851,9,5,9007,31989,27235,1111,30078}

**`30078` is live for both.** Staying `in_progress` because the rest of the done-when — "a browser session can sign a `kind:30078` through `/sign` without a `kind_not_allowed` refusal" — needs a person signed in as themselves. I hold only the agent's credential.

## The unverified note in this ticket is now answered

It said the read confirming production matched `seed.ts` was blocked. I ran it **before** re-seeding, and production matched the file exactly — no drift, for any of the four credentials. Worth knowing because the worry has been carried in the ROADMAP for a while and can now be dropped.

The pre-seed state, for the record:

    claude-agent  {27235,1851,9,30851,30850,9007}
    estiva-peek   {9,7,5,9007,27235,1851,9101,9002,9008}
    estiva-ship   {30850,30851,1851,9,5,9007,31989,27235}
    pc-0a4935     {27235}

## The agent question this ticket asks

It says to decide `30078` for `claude-agent` deliberately rather than by omission. **Not granted, and the answer is probably no on the merits** — read state records that a person read something, and a bot marking things read is a reading nobody did. It is also mechanically separate: `claude-agent` is not in `SEED_APPS`, so it would take the admin PATCH route rather than a seed line.

Note the running image already contained the merged seed before I re-seeded — the update timer had pulled it — so the "re-seed ran before the pull" failure did not apply this time. I checked by grepping `dist/db/seed.js` inside the container rather than comparing image ids, which is a cheaper check and answers the same question.

@Katerina Kelepouri If you want, you can try it as well now, but it would requires some tedious command line magic on your side.

What it does now is that it allows you to read data from Ship. So you could say something like "Check ticket PEE-1 and implement it".

Alternatively, I will on Friday and over the weekend finish the whole thing including better onboarding to this feature without command lines.

@estiva-app/ui 0.3.0 published and verified from the registry — displayNode is in the shipped .d.ts.

ship#80 is out of draft with CI green (check + web, 228 tests), estiva-agent#32 green. Both ready, and they must merge together — one fold, two repos, one recorded state.

That leaves only Ship writing block documents, which I proposed as its own slice.

@Katerina Kelepouri Don't need to read the AI slop above. But this is mostly working. It's part of my attempts to unify and improve the cross messaging experience. Two main things:
- Now even Ship has the notion of unread messages. The styling is not great, but it's there.
- If you read a message in Ship then it's automatically marked for you as read in Peek as well.

